# Caddyfile — the Vector Sigma master-device TLS edge (fleet-ops-f57.13,
# rewritten for automated rotation — see docs/tls-runbook.md).
#
# Fronts the whole composition:
#   https://<master-hostname>:443  -> registrar (admin console + /v1 API)
#   https://<master-hostname>:8443  -> litellm (gateway /ui, /v1/*, /a2a/*,
#                                     /health/*)
#   https://<master-hostname>:8444  -> scotty queue UI (basic_auth)
#
# NOT fronted (deliberate, per the Cabal precedent — LAN-internal services
# stay off the edge):
#   postgres, litellm-init — compose-internal only.
#   dolt (when the queue bead lands)  — the fleet's shared queue DB is
#     fleet-internal traffic; it gets no edge exposure. The Scotty UI (when
#     it lands) is fronted here — see the scotty stanza below.
#
# Site address: the hostname arrives as the TLS_HOSTNAME environment
# variable (a balenaCloud fleet variable on the device, --env-file value in
# the deploy/ self-host example). Caddy performs {$VAR:default} Caddyfile
# substitution itself at adapt time, so the same baked file serves both
# worlds with no compose-interpolation fork (the balena supervisor does
# none).
#
# TLS: `tls internal` — Caddy's own built-in local CA (the `pki` app
# below), self-provisioned on first boot and stored on the caddy-data
# volume. No owner-run script, no fleet-variable cert pair, no certs-init
# service: Caddy mints AND renews the leaf itself, automatically, for as
# long as the volume persists. The CA's root_cn is pinned to "Vector Sigma
# Internal CA" so the fleet keeps its own identity in served certs (not
# Caddy's generic default) — devices still trust it via VS_CA_CERT_B64,
# extracted once from the caddy-data volume (docs/tls-runbook.md).
#
# MagicDNS overlay fronting (j7g phase 1b, fleet-ops-lrb): each fronted
# site gains the master's MagicDNS FQDN as a SECOND site address —
# provision-then-flip (the f57.9 hazard class): caddy must serve the
# name (site alias + internal-CA leaf) BEFORE any device's
# REGISTRAR_URL flips to it. This ships one release AHEAD of the
# fleet-wide variable flip; the AC2 pre-flip canary (lrb thread,
# 2026-10-02) proved the pre-provision state — TLS alert 80 on
# 443/8443 for the MagicDNS SNI — and these aliases are the fix.
# Same blocks, same handlers: the fronting is provably identical on
# both names.
#
# The MagicDNS name arrives as the TS_MASTER_DNS environment variable
# (a balenaCloud fleet variable on the device, --env-file value in the
# deploy/ self-host example): the master's FULL MagicDNS FQDN, e.g.
# vector-sigma.tailb7207e.ts.net. Same shape as TLS_HOSTNAME: the
# {$TS_MASTER_DNS:vector-sigma.tailb7207e.ts.net} default mirrors the
# structural compose env value, and the fleet variable wins when set.
# The name is STRUCTURAL, not a secret — the phase-1b extra_hosts pins
# already carry it literally in both balena composes (extra_hosts has
# no variable path under the supervisor; the lrb canary DECIDED that
# mechanism), so baking the same default here adds no new exposure;
# the tailnet itself is ACL-gated.
#
# In-container resolution of the MagicDNS name is pinned via
# extra_hosts in the consuming services' compose stanzas (the lrb
# canary verdict: the container DNS chain — embedded 127.0.0.11 ->
# host dnsmasq -> upstream — has no ts.net route, and accept-dns
# cannot reach the host dnsmasq chain from tailscaled's own mount
# namespace; the static pin is the DECIDED flip mechanism —
# docs/tailscale-runbook.md § phase 1b).
{
	pki {
		ca local {
			root_cn "Vector Sigma Internal CA"
			intermediate_cn "Vector Sigma Internal CA - Intermediate"
		}
	}
}

# --- port 80: redirect to TLS (one site for the whole hostname) -----------
# A catch-all :80 site follows the hostname-specific one (Copilot f57.13
# review): a request arriving through a balenaCloud public URL carries the
# public hostname in Host, not TLS_HOSTNAME, and must still receive the
# 308 — the README/runbook promise port 80 ALWAYS redirects.
http://{$TLS_HOSTNAME:vsigma.lan}:80 {
	redir https://{$TLS_HOSTNAME:vsigma.lan}{uri} 308
}

http://:80 {
	redir https://{host}{uri} 308
}

# --- registrar: admin console + /v1 API on 443 ----------------------------
{$TLS_HOSTNAME:vsigma.lan}, {$TS_MASTER_DNS:vector-sigma.tailb7207e.ts.net} {
	tls internal

	# The registrar derives nothing from X-Forwarded-* today (LAN-only
	# trust model); TRUST_PROXY stays a registrar-side opt-in. Caddy sets
	# X-Forwarded-For/Proto by default — the registrar ignores them unless
	# enabled.
	reverse_proxy registrar:3000
}

# --- VS gateway (LiteLLM): gateway + /ui + /a2a/* on 8443 -----------------
{$TLS_HOSTNAME:vsigma.lan}:8443, {$TS_MASTER_DNS:vector-sigma.tailb7207e.ts.net}:8443 {
	tls internal

	# LiteLLM derives agent-card URLs from the request Host header;
	# reverse_proxy preserves it by default, so card URLs stay correct
	# (https://<hostname>:8443) with no PROXY_BASE_URL override needed.
	reverse_proxy litellm:4000
}

# --- Scotty queue UI: fronted behind the TLS edge on 8444 (f57.15 + 77i) --
# Was raw unauthenticated HTTP on host :3306 — the composition's widest
# exposure. Now: same internal-CA pattern as the other fronted services,
# PLUS basic_auth (D2 green-lit — covers an ACL misconfiguration on an
# otherwise unauthenticated read-only UI).
#
# The bcrypt hash arrives as the SCOTTY_BASIC_AUTH_HASH fleet variable
# (owner-minted via `caddy hash-password --plaintext`; the README table).
# NO default: an unset/empty variable fails caddy's adapt LOUDLY at boot
# — a deliberate fail-loud gate (LITELLM_MASTER_KEY precedent: the
# variable is set on the fleet BEFORE the release carrying this stanza
# is tagged). Never a plaintext or literal hash in this file.
#
# Upstream is the in-container scotty:3306 (compose network) — NOT the
# drifted scotty:8080 from the old placeholder.
{$TLS_HOSTNAME:vsigma.lan}:8444, {$TS_MASTER_DNS:vector-sigma.tailb7207e.ts.net}:8444 {
	tls internal
	basic_auth {
		owner {$SCOTTY_BASIC_AUTH_HASH}
	}
	reverse_proxy scotty:3306
}
